e  amount of water that can soak into the  ground in those places because if you  spill a cup of water on a street it  doesn't go into the ground the same way  as if you spilled it in a forest and so  more water reaches the streams in city  landscapes and that causes a lot more  flooding and a lot more erosion and can  bring in a lot more  pollution so now what what city planners  and water managers and concerned  citizens are trying to do is find ways  to put more water back into the ground  through things like rain gardens and  rain barrels and you know not washing  your car on the driveway so things like  that so there's a project up in  Cleveland where Cleveland Metroparks got  money to put in a whole bunch of rain  gardens up and down a couple of streets  and so my student and I are looking at  the data collected from the storm sewers  below the streets so if it rains on the  street some of the water goes into the  storm sewer Network and then hopefully  some of it goes into the rain gardens so  we're looking at one Street where there  are no rain gardens and one Street where  there are rain gardens and trying to see  how much of an effect those rain gardens  are having on terms of the water that's  getting into the storm sewers and  ultimately down into the street so your  research could really change how people  end up behaving and how people end up  using their water that would be the  long-term big goal it certainly wouldn't  be from anyone science project or any  one researcher alone but hopefully we  can add a little bit more information  that helps people improve the way we  design cities or the way we do stream  restoration and then other scientists  can add another little bit of  information and eventually all that  would get translated into engineering  practice and urban design cool so um no  now you told me a little bit about what  you're doing with the actual research  once you're done say comparing like one  straight one street that has rain  borough barrels versus one that does not  what happens when you have the answers  to those questions and the result of  those of that research what's the  process after that um well I think  there's two answers to that one is that  we never really have the answers to the  questions usually if we have answered  one question we've created like five  more um so maybe now if we know how  effective putting 20 rain barrels on a  single Street was our question might  become okay well can we put enough rain  barrels in a whole watershed in a whole  drainage area to make  a real difference in the stream so it  becomes sort of a it's like building  something out of Legos you know once you  put one block on then you've got space  to put more on um so that's one part of  it so science never really ends the  other part is that it's from getting an  answer and and me and my student knowing  the answer doesn't change anything so  what we have to do is communicate our  science to other scientists and the way  we do that is by writing articles and  publishing them in scientific journals  and going to conferences and talking  about them but in my field we also have  to talk to people like the regulators  that are setting up the way we design  streets or the way we manage stormwater  and we have to talk to homeowners and we  have to talk to all sorts of people that  aren't just other scientists so for  example yesterday my students and I were  at a conference called the National  nonpoint source monitoring conference  which is a lot of big words but it was  basically people from all over the  country some scientists some people who  work for cities and states and some  people who work for companies we're all  very interested in figuring out how we  can have less pollution getting in to  streams and groundwater and sharing  stories about the things that they were  trying and how well they were working  and how well maybe they weren't working  maybe lessons learned one way or the  other and so talking to people and  groups like that is a great way of  making science actually translate into  action that's really cool so I actually  majan that all this does not happen for  free you don't just go out and you know  we're putting rain barrels for free so  when you get the money to do your  research and how do you get it how much  does your research costs and you know  where do you get that money and how you  go about getting the money to do your  work that is a tough question because  you're right research doesn't happen for  free and so one of the big parts of my  job is asking for money to do the  science and it turns out that i have to  ask for money and get a lot of answers  no before i get an answer yes so some of  the places that i can get money  to do my science or things like the US  National Science Foundation or the  Environmental Protection Agency but  unfortunately there's a lot more science  than needs to be done then there's money  to fund it so like I said I get no for  an answer an awful lot um you would  think with a really applied field like  stream restoration and stormwater  management that there would be more  money around but oftentimes that money  is for doing things like buying the rain  barrels and not necessarily for doing  things like figuring out how effective  the rain barrels actually are the  science end of it so sometimes I have to  sort of get creative and and like for  example this rain gardens project were  sort of doing it for free Cleveland  Metroparks got the money to do the rain  garden installations and they got it  from the Environmental Protection Agency  and we're kind of adding the science on  there and in exchange my graduate  student will get a job hopefully with  Cleveland Metroparks at least for next  summer so there's lots of little little  bits of money that might be available um  but you know it's a continual challenge  to try to get projects done and some  projects require a lot of equipment and  streams or in groundwater and those can  be expensive but even projects where not  a lot of equipments required still  requires time from people and so  supporting graduate students and  undergraduate students to do the  research is one of the things I'm always  trying to find money to do it's good to  be realistic so can you tell me what a  typical days like for you when you're  conducting research you know when you're  actually out in the field or analyzing  data what is the day like for you doing  again there's no typical day there's  sort of three types of days there's the  days entirely in front of a computer  there's the days that are mostly in the  lab and then there are the days that are  in the field and the field days are  probably the most interesting so i guess  i'll tell you about those um and i can  tell you about some of the days that i  spent the summer in that stream that was  being about  be restored and we were sorta  frantically trying to collect data about  the stream because we knew that in a  matter of days or weeks bulldozers would  arrive and start to really just not  destroy the stream but completely  recreate it um so we would go out in the  morning and in in hip waders and and we  would do things like use survey  equipment to try and get really good  measurements of the channel shape or  there was one whole day where we pounded  about inch and a half PVC pipes into the  bottom of the stream and those pipes had  holes on them in the bottom and so  they're what we call pedometers but  basically what they do is they tell us  about water that's moving from the  stream and into the stream bed and so we  pound these pipes into the ground and  then we'd wait for them to kind of  settle out and then we'd add more water  to the top of the pipe and we watch how  quickly it disappeared back out of the  pipe and that tells us how easily water  is moving into those stream bed  sediments so if it's moving quickly than  there's into it there's a good  connection between the stream and the  groundwater and if it's moving really  slowly there's not and it turns out a  lot of really cool biology and chemistry  that happen in stream beds but they  happen because of them mixing between  the surface water or the stream water  and the ground water and so if it's  really hard to get that mixing to happen  because it's like lots of really tiny  clay particles and silt particles then  you don't get as much biological  diversity in there you don't get as much  of the biogeochemical processing which  is a fancy way of saying all the  nutrients that living things need to  live there's a lot of fancy chemistry  happening in the stream bed so I'm  trying to figure out the physical  aspects of it that'll really be kind of  the big controls on biogeochemistry in  the biology that's happening there so  yeah pounding pipes and a stream beds  and pouring water into those pipes and  then making careful measurements of how  quickly things disappear and then we had  a really long fun day where we actually  turn the stream purple so  we had a dog we had to die that we  injected into the stream so basically we  had a big trash can full of very very  purple water um and and it's completely  biologically harmless but what happens  is that a lot of it just gets moved down  in the stream water itself but some of  it moves again into the bottom of the  stream and into the bed of the stream  where all the cool biology and  biogeochemistry is happening and that  when it's moving through the stream bed  it's moving much more slowly and biology  is happening down there and this die is  super cool because when it is  metabolized basically when microbes use  it it goes from being a purple dye to  being a pink dye and so we can actually  take bottles of water in various places  in the street and at various times when  we're adding the purple dye and we can  take them back to the lab and we can  actually measure how much purple dye is  in that water and how much pink dye is  in that water and so we can measure from  that we can sort of take it back to our  computer then this is multi-day process  take it back to our computer and figure  out how much exchange there is between  the stream bed in the stream water and  how much metabolic activity or how much  biology is really going on in the stream  bed so that was a super fun day  scientifically there was lots of energy  going into hauling big tubs of water  around and getting things set up and  then once we got the experiment going i  had five four helpers sitting in various  places along the stream was basically  reading a book most the time but every  20 minutes or so grabbing a bottle of  water from the stream and saving it for  laboratory use later but the net result  is that you really did turn a stream  purple for science we really did turn a  stream purple for science and it was it  was pretty brutal this is in a city park  and we tried to get going early in the  morning but it was still pretty purple  by the time evening rolled around and it  was a nice  summer day and there was lots of all  fields and we definitely had some people  wander by and one of the my assistants  said these two women were talking to  each other and I said the stream looks  really weird today I wonder what's going  on so usually if I hear a comment like  that I try and give people sort of a  quick explanation of the science we're  doing but I wasn't a way as possible the  kids were fascinated by the purple  stream so that was really fun but we  cool so can you tell me you know it  sounds like you have a really awesome  job you get to turn streams purple for  science um but you also get to do a lot  of teaching and stuff how did you get to  where you are now like how did you get  to your current job you know where did  you start what were your prophecies that  you know mujo develop your career the  weighted um so actually I would credit a  lot of my start and science to science  fairs because i started doing science  fair projects in fifth grade like many  kids do but i kept on doing them through  metal school in high school and in that  process I discovered that I really liked  rivers um so my first science fair  project was on dish washing and the  chemistry of dishwashing and not taught  me two things I didn't like dishwashing  and I was not that into chemistry you  had to be taught that you didn't enjoy  dishwasher I think it was my mom's  excuse to get me to do um and then I did  one on snow which was pretty cool and  then I started working on rocks and  rocks were pretty they were even cooler  than snow but you know they don't really  change very fast and I grew up in the  middle of North America where the rocks  are really old they don't even have very  exciting fossils and them but I also  grew up right on the Mississippi River  and I grew up in a town on the  Mississippi River during some big floods  and so one year when I was trying to  figure out what I was going to do with  rocks for a science fair project these  big floods were going on and so we  couldn't get  the rocks we wanted to get to on the  banks of the river so I ended up working  on the river itself instead and and then  I was basically [ __ ] because um here  was something that was changing all the  time it was really important to my  community you know people lots of people  would like to go out and fish and boat  on the river but also there was a lot of  our economy was tied to the river and so  these floods were affecting people and  and the sediment that was moving through  the river was changing where the fishing  holes were how easily the boats could  move up and down river and it was just  compared to the rocks that were sitting  there and not really changing for  hundreds of millions of years the river  was super exciting um and so I started  doing fines for projects on Mississippi  River and I actually got to go to ICF  the International Science and  Engineering Fair 